          Programs that fault, and the trap of testing them bare          ======================================================Three programs stopped with    Exit code: E_PRCABT(228) Process Aborted    Exception: pid=2 vector=$02 err=#000:102Vector $02 on a 68000 is a bus error: the program read an address the machinewould not give it.  os9exec kills the process, so nothing is printed and theprogram looks simply dead.    firq        FIRQ utility    souper      text utility    sysmem      show system memory useALL THREE HAVE SINCE LEFT THE DISK -- they are system utilities writtenat Microware and they do not ship here.  The reading is kept because itis the answer for any program of that shape, and because the warningunder it is worth more than the three programs were.That list was five until it was checked properly.  `aterm` and `snake` were onit, and both are fine.READ THIS BEFORE BELIEVING ANY OF IT------------------------------------A program run as os9exec's FIRST process has no environment at all -- no TERM,no TERMCAP, no PATH.  Nothing on this disk is used that way.  `SYS/login` iswhat sets TERM and TERMCAP, and 69 programs here read TERMCAP.Run bare, `aterm` and `snake` took bus errors and `cribbage` and `crib`printed "Environment variable TERM not defined" and aborted.  Run inside alogin session -- `bash /dd/SYS/login`, then the program -- all four work:`aterm` prints its banner, `snake` draws its board, and both cribbages askwhether you would like the instructions.So the same missing TERMCAP surfaces as a clean error message in one programand as a bus error in another, depending on whether its terminal librarychecks the pointer before following it.  A bus error is not evidence that aprogram is broken.  It may only be evidence that you ran it wrong.Host environment variables do not help: setting TERM in the shell that startsos9exec changes nothing, because os9exec builds the OS-9 environment itself.Going through `SYS/login` is the only way.The three that fault before their first system call---------------------------------------------------`firq`, `souper` and `sysmem` fail in a login session exactly as they do bare,and they fault at the same place in the same shared startup code, on the sameinstruction:    MOVEA.L (A0,$004c),A4      A0 = $AAAAAAA4They have made no system call at all when it happens -- os9exec reports`Last syscall: START`.  $AAAAAAA4 is not a value any program computed; it isos9exec's own fill pattern for memory and registers it has not set.  So thesethree read a pointer out of A0 at entry, and under os9exec A0 holds fill.ANSWERED 2026-08-19, and the answer is that the programs are at fault.Microware's OS-9/68000 Technical Reference Manual v2.4 documents exactly whatF$Fork hands a new process, and it lists A0 first:    (a0) = undefined              (a3) = primary (forked) module pointer    (a1) = top of memory pointer  (a5) = parameter pointer    (a2) = undefined              (a6) = static storage base pointer    (a4) = undefined              (a7) = stack pointerA0 is UNDEFINED at entry.  os9exec is entitled to leave its fill patternthere, and a program that follows A0 before setting it is reading a registerOS-9 never promised it anything in.  Note that the pointer these three seemto want -- the forked module -- is passed in A3, not A0.But that is only the surface.  The real reason is in their source, which ison this disk at SRC/hc_utils:    @_sysattr: equ $a001   NOTE: this code must run in system (supervisor) state    #include <sysglob.h>    #define GLOB(var)(_sysglob->var)   /* process access to sys globals */All three are SYSTEM-STATE programs.  They ask to run in supervisor state andthey read the kernel's system globals directly.  firq's own header commentsays "you must make firq as a super user".  os9exec runs them as ordinaryuser-state processes, so the globals pointer they expect is not there.They also cannot be rebuilt here.  `sysglob.h' is Microware's -- it is in theSDK and it is deliberately not on this disk -- and it is the kernel's owninternal layout, which is the category Microware most needs kept unpublished.So they would not have run under os9exec whatever was done to them, andthey have since left the disk with the rest of the Microware-authoredutilities.  That is a complete answer, not a gap.  The rest of that suite-- `sysid`, `sysmax` and `sysmin` -- ran perfectly well, so the suite as awhole was never the problem, only this one code path; they went with theother three for the same reason, which is authorship and not behaviour.One that looked like it wanted floating-point hardware, and did not-------------------------------------------------------------------This section used to say that two programs execute floating-pointinstructions with no coprocessor underneath.  One of them is not aprogram, and the other was not doing that:  os9lib    is the RTF Fortran RUN-TIME LIBRARY, not a program.  Running a            library AS a program is what reached its floating-point code.            See DOC/README-FORTRAN; it is not a failure.  config    printed char, short, int, long, pointer and FLOAT properties            correctly, then aborted on vector $0B -- the 68000's line-F            emulator vector, which is how a coprocessor instruction            announces that there is no coprocessor.  It died where            `double` began.  IT RUNS TO ITS LAST LINE NOW.THE VECTOR SAID COPROCESSOR AND THE CAUSE WAS SOMETHING ELSE ENTIRELY.`config` INSTALLS ITS OWN HANDLER for that vector: it means to catch theexception and do the arithmetic in software, which is exactly why itneeds no coprocessor.  The handler was going in at the wrong address.  AnF$STrap table gives each handler as a word offset from the table entry,SIGNED, reaching plus or minus 32K -- so a table placed after the routinesit names carries a negative one -- and reading that word unsigned puts thehandler 64K above the routine.  The process then dies at the veryexception it asked to be told about.  Corrected in the emulator on2026-09-12, and measured here by running `config` on the build before thatchange and the build with it.SO THE LESSON IS THE ONE THIS FILE OPENS WITH.  A fault on the vector forthe very thing a program is doing is not evidence that the hardware ismissing, any more than a bus error is evidence that a program is broken.A program that installs a handler and then dies on that handler's ownvector is a program whose handler did not go where it asked.For the record, since this was written down as a reason to ask for more:Microware's `fpu` module is the floating-point emulator for a machine withno 68881, and `p2init` is what installs it as a system extension.  Thepermission this collection asked for and received covers cio, math,math881, csl and csl020, and NOTHING HERE NEEDS MORE THAN THAT -- `config`was the concrete case for asking about `fpu`, and it turned out not to beone.Five SNOBOL4 games -- FIXED 2026-08-19, they play------------------------------------------------  poker  blackjak  rpoem  rstory  stoneThese faulted with `Illegal instruction: 4afc', which is both the 68000ILLEGAL opcode and the OS-9 module sync word -- so control had jumped intoa module header.  That looked like a corrupt library and was not.Each opens a SYNTAX FILE by a bare relative name (PHRASE.SYN, RPOEM.SYN,RSTORY.SYN, RSTORY.ACT).  The open failed, the null result was used as apattern tree, and the first match call jumped through a null functionpointer.  The files existed all along, in DOC/snobol, which is notsomewhere a running program looks.They now live in GAMES/SNOBOL and the five were rebuilt fromSRC/effo_snobol to name them there.  A `chd' would not have been enough:bash cannot change the OS-9 data directory and sh cannot fork an absolutepathname, so no user could have satisfied the old relative open.`tformat' always worked because it does not use the pattern matcher andopens no syntax file.  So did `rstory2', which was dropped from thecollection on 2026-09-12 for an unrelated reason: the four story programsit forks were never distributed.About aterm-----------`aterm` works.  It is 100% 68000 assembly, and the collection now includesits configuration:    SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Ctl     control file, 20 sequences, ANSI/VT-100 codes    SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Phone   empty dialler list, ready to fill in    SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Print   printer scriptThe control file was written from the example in the program's own manual, withthe VT-52 escape sequences of the original replaced by ANSI ones and the devicedriver name set to `term`.  `DOC/aterm/ATerm.Doc` is the full manual and`SRC/aterm/ATerm.a` the complete source.  To do anything useful it still wantsa real serial port: `aterm /t1`.